---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../consts';

// Privacy policy. Lives on waffled.app (NOT the docs subdomain) because Google's
// OAuth brand verification wants the policy on the same host as the home page it
// was given. Keep the "Google user data" section in sync with the scopes in
// apps/api/src/platform/config.ts — a reviewer compares them line for line.
const updated = 'September 1, 2026';
---

<Base
	title="Privacy policy — Waffled"
	description="How Waffled handles your data: what a self-hosted install stores, what the iOS app collects, and exactly what Google account data the calendar integration accesses and why."
>
	<section class="legal">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="legal-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">Legal</span>
				<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
				<p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>
			</div>

			<div class="legal-body">
				<div class="legal-callout">
					<p><strong>The short version.</strong> Waffled is self-hosted software. You run it on your own
					server, and your household's data — calendar, chores, meals, lists, photos — lives in
					<em>your</em> database on <em>your</em> hardware. We do not operate a service that receives it,
					we cannot see it, and there is no account to sign up for. When you connect Google Calendar,
					your data flows directly between Google and your own server.</p>
					<p>We do not sell data, we do not serve ads, and we do not use your data to train AI models.</p>
				</div>

				<h2>Who this policy covers</h2>
				<p>This policy is published by the maintainers of <strong>Waffled</strong>, the open-source family
				hub distributed at <a href={LINKS.github} rel="noopener">github.com/kevinpsites/waffled</a> and as
				the <a href={LINKS.appStore} rel="noopener">Waffled iOS app</a>. It explains how the Waffled
				software handles personal data, including data obtained from Google APIs.</p>

				<p>Because Waffled is self-hosted, there are two distinct roles:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>You, the operator</strong> — the person who installs Waffled on a server they
					control. You decide what data goes in, who in your household can see it, how it is backed up,
					and when it is deleted. Under laws such as the GDPR you are the <em>data controller</em> for
					your own installation.</li>
					<li><strong>Us, the maintainers</strong> — we write and publish the software. We do not host it
					for you, we receive no copy of your household's data, and we have no technical means of
					accessing your server.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Data your Waffled server stores</h2>
				<p>A Waffled install stores whatever your household puts into it, in a PostgreSQL database on your
				own machine. That typically includes household member names and avatars, login credentials
				(passwords are hashed, never stored in plain text), calendar events, chores and reward balances,
				meal plans and recipes, shopping and to-do lists, pantry items, personal goals and their progress,
				and photos you upload for the screensaver.</p>
				<p>None of this is transmitted to us or to any third party. It is sent only between your own server
				and the devices in your household that you have signed in — the kitchen kiosk, phones and tablets.
				Deleting the data, or the whole database, is entirely under your control.</p>

				<h2>Google user data</h2>
				<p>Waffled includes an <strong>optional</strong> Google Calendar integration. It is off until
				someone in your household explicitly connects a Google account in Settings, and it can be
				disconnected at any time.</p>

				<h3>What we request, and why</h3>
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr><th>Scope</th><th>What it grants</th><th>Why Waffled needs it</th></tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>openid</code>, <code>email</code></td>
							<td>Your Google account identifier and email address.</td>
							<td>To label the connected account in the interface ("Connected as you@gmail.com") and
							to recognise the same account on reconnect, so connecting twice doesn't create a
							duplicate.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar</code></td>
							<td>Read and write access to the calendars on your Google account.</td>
							<td><strong>Read</strong> — to list your calendars so you can choose which ones to show,
							and to import their events onto the shared family calendar and kitchen display.
							<strong>Write</strong> — so an event created or edited on the family calendar or kiosk is
							saved back to your Google calendar, which is the entire point of a two-way family
							calendar.</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<h3>What is stored, and where</h3>
				<p>Everything below is written to <em>your own</em> Waffled database. None of it reaches the
				maintainers or any third-party service.</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Your Google account id and email address</strong> — to identify the connection.</li>
					<li><strong>OAuth tokens</strong> — the refresh token, and a short-lived cached access token, are
					encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM using an encryption key generated on your own server.</li>
					<li><strong>Calendar metadata</strong> — each calendar's name, description, time zone, colour and
					access role, so you can pick which ones to display.</li>
					<li><strong>Event details</strong> for the calendars you chose to sync — title, description,
					location, start and end times, and Google's event identifiers — so they can be displayed on the
					family calendar and kept in sync.</li>
				</ul>

				<h3>How it is used</h3>
				<p>Google data is used for one purpose only: displaying and editing your calendar inside your own
				Waffled installation, a feature that is prominent and visible in the app's interface. It is never
				used for advertising, never sold or transferred to anyone, never read by a human at Waffled (we
				have no access to it), and never used to train machine-learning or AI models.</p>

				<div class="legal-callout">
					<p><strong>Limited Use disclosure.</strong> Waffled's use and transfer of information received
					from Google APIs to any other app will adhere to the
					<a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" rel="noopener">Google
					API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements.</p>
				</div>

				<h3>Retention and deletion</h3>
				<p>Synced calendar data stays in your database until you remove it. Disconnecting the Google
				account in <strong>Settings → Calendar</strong> deletes the stored tokens and removes the synced
				calendars and their events from your server. Deleting your Waffled installation, or its database,
				removes everything.</p>

				<h3>Revoking access</h3>
				<p>You can revoke Waffled's access to your Google account at any time, independently of Waffled, at
				<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" rel="noopener">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>.
				Doing so immediately stops any further syncing.</p>

				<h2>Other optional integrations</h2>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Microsoft / Outlook calendar</strong> — works the same way as Google Calendar, with
					the same storage, encryption and deletion behaviour, against Microsoft's APIs.</li>
					<li><strong>Apple Health (iPhone only)</strong> — if you link a goal to a Health metric, the iOS
					app reads that metric on your device and sends only the resulting progress number (for example
					"7,340 steps") to your own server. Raw health records never leave your iPhone and are never sent
					to us.</li>
					<li><strong>Open Food Facts</strong> — scanning a barcode in the pantry sends that barcode to
					the public <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org/" rel="noopener">Open Food Facts</a>
					database to look up the product. No personal or household information is included.</li>
					<li><strong>AI capture bar</strong> — off by default. If you enable it and configure a model,
					the text you type into the capture bar is sent to the AI provider <em>you</em> chose and
					configured, under that provider's privacy policy. Running a local model keeps it on your own
					hardware.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>The iOS app</h2>
				<p>The Waffled iOS app talks only to the server address you enter when you sign in. It contains no
				analytics SDKs, no advertising SDKs and no third-party trackers, and it sends no data to the
				maintainers. Authentication tokens are stored in the iOS Keychain on your device.</p>

				<h2>This website</h2>
				<p>waffled.app and docs.waffled.app are static sites. They set no tracking cookies and run no
				analytics or advertising scripts. Our hosting provider processes standard server request logs
				(such as IP address and user agent) for security and reliability, as any web server does. Web
				fonts are loaded from Google Fonts, which receives the request as part of serving the font
				files.</p>

				<h2>Children</h2>
				<p>Waffled is designed for households, and a household often includes children. Any information
				about children in a Waffled install is entered by that household's own adults and stays on that
				household's server — we never receive it, and we do not knowingly collect personal information
				from children ourselves.</p>

				<h2>Security</h2>
				<p>Waffled hashes passwords, encrypts stored third-party OAuth tokens at rest with AES-256-GCM,
				and expects to be served over HTTPS. Because you host it, the security of your installation — the
				server, its network exposure, its backups — is ultimately in your hands; our
				<a href={LINKS.install} rel="noopener">installation docs</a> cover the recommended setup. Report
				vulnerabilities via our <a href={LINKS.security} rel="noopener">security policy</a>.</p>

				<h2>Your rights</h2>
				<p>Because your data lives in a database you control, you already have direct access to it: you can
				export it, correct it, or delete it yourself at any time, and our
				<a href={LINKS.docs} rel="noopener">documentation</a> explains how to back up and restore it. We
				hold no copy of it, so there is nothing for us to disclose or erase on your behalf. If your
				household's Waffled instance is run by someone else, direct requests about your data to that
				operator.</p>

				<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
				<p>If this policy changes materially we will update the date at the top of this page and note the
				change in the project's <a href={LINKS.changelog} rel="noopener">changelog</a>.</p>

				<h2>Contact</h2>
				<p>Questions about this policy, or about how Waffled handles Google user data, can be raised as an
				issue at <a href={LINKS.issues} rel="noopener">github.com/kevinpsites/waffled/issues</a>, which is
				the maintainers' monitored contact channel for the project.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
